---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const title = "Technology Adoption and Learning Preferences for Older Adults: Evolving Perceptions, Ongoing Challenges, and Emerging Design Opportunities";
---
<BaseLayout title={title}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="meta">My take on the paper.</p>

  <div class="paper">
    <p>
      The adoption of technology by older adults has increased, as discussed in the paper; [2] however, my question is whether the technology is adapting to the needs of the older adult population, especially in light of AI-generated content, false information, and fake customer service numbers. Given the growing scams in the digital space, especially with older adults, [1] this can be added in future work and potentially implemented in the Help Kiosk to help the ageing population tackle this issue.
    </p>

    <p>
      My other concern is the diversity of accessibility needs that were also tested. The Help Kiosk might work for people who have vision and upper mobility, but if the goal is to provide independence to older adults, it is important to cover a variety of age-acquired disabilities like BLV due to cataracts, acquired immobility, or loss of hearing.
    </p>

    <p>
      It would have also been more insightful if income was noted because the first group of people mentioned going to webinars to learn and being up to date with the technology, which can also be attributed to better work-life balance, stable income, or living in urban areas. The study could benefit from dividing the demographic by income level, as this could influence access to ATs. It could have also influenced the design approach to the Help Kiosk.
    </p>

    <p>
      Another specification in the paper that would have helped is whether the Help Kiosk is available in other languages, such as French.
    </p>

    <p style="text-align: center;">
      Reference:
    </p>

    <p>
      [1] Bailey J, Taylor L, Kingston P, Watts G (2021), “Older adults and “scams”: evidence from the Mass Observation Archive”. The Journal of Adult Protection, Vol. 23 No. 1 pp. 57–69, doi: https://doi.org/10.1108/JAP-07-2020-0030
    </p>

    <p style=”text-align: center;”>
      Citation:
    </p>

    <p>
      Carolyn Pang, Zhiqin Collin Wang, Joanna McGrenere, Rock Leung, Jiamin Dai, and Karyn Moffatt. 2021. Technology Adoption and Learning Preferences for Older Adults: Evolving Perceptions, Ongoing Challenges, and Emerging Design Opportunities. In Proc. of the 2021 CHI Conf. on Human Factors in Computing Systems (CHI '21). ACM, New York, NY, USA, Article 490, 1–13. https://doi.org/10.1145/3411764.3445702
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
